            <ArticleTitle>Relationship between carbon dioxide levels and reported headaches on the international space station.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>477-83</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/JOM.0000000000000158</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Because of anecdotal reports of CO(2)-related symptoms onboard the International Space Station (ISS), the relationship between CO(2) and in-flight headaches was analyzed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Headache reports and CO(2) measurements were obtained, and arithmetic means and single-point maxima were determined for 24-hour and 7-day periods. Multiple imputation addressed missing data, and logistic regression modeled the relationship between CO(2), headache probability, and covariates.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">CO(2) level, age at launch, time in-flight, and data source were significantly associated with headache. For each 1-mm Hg increase in CO(2), the odds of a crew member reporting a headache doubled. To keep the risk of headache below 1%, average 7-day CO(2) would need to be maintained below 2.5 mm Hg (current ISS range: 1 to 9 mm Hg).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Although headache incidence was not high, results suggest an increased susceptibility to physiological effects of CO(2) in-flight.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
